  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is implemented and tested. I judged this from the supplied inputs only and did not run the tests.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs`, `Requirements` arm: it skips every definition whose kind is `Term` and keeps only names that match the glob. Requirements and examples are therefore selected and named definitions are excluded. The `matches` list holds only those names.\n- `terms::dependencies` in `src/terms.rs` is separate from glob matching.\n  - For `Requirements` inputs it scans the bodies of the selected blocks for `{term}` references.\n  - It adds each referenced term, and the terms those terms reference, as separate definition inputs, whatever their names are.\n  - A term is therefore never pulled in because its name shares the glob's prefix. A term is only a dependency if some selected text references it.\n  - A missing target gives a \"no matching named definition\" error.\n- `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (line 475) calls `dependencies` when capturing inputs, and `validate_dependencies` calls it during validation.\n- The other consumers apply the same exclusion. `status_markdown_with_options`, `info_markdown` and `preprocess_book` filter out `Term` definitions or build evidence-only definition sets.\n\n**Tests**\n- `globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob` in `tests/definitions.rs` cites both `requirements.definitions.selection` and `requirements.definitions.glob_dependency`.\n  - It captures `rules.*` and appends an unreferenced definition `rules.unrelated`, which matches the glob by name.\n  - It asserts the first input's `matches` is exactly `[\"rules.ready\"]`, so the definition is not selected.\n  - It asserts `glossary.a` and `glossary.b` are captured as separate `definition` inputs although they don't match `rules.*`.\n  - It asserts the status output does not mention `rules.unrelated`.\n- This matches the example text: a referenced definition outside the glob is captured, and an unreferenced definition with a matching name is not.\n- Supporting tests in `tests/definitions.rs` cover transitive dependencies: the input count is 3, a missing definition input is rejected, changed definition text is detected, and the Rust-item path is covered. An example whose input is a requirement picks up its term dependencies, and `info` displays them.\n- `tests/commands.rs` covers the CLI side: `status`/`info` globs, \"no definitions match\", and an invalid `--requirements` glob.\n\n**Gaps:** none significant. No FIXMEs are involved.",
